        <body>
            <div xml:id="d206">

                <pb facs="0486" n="390" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg390"/>

                <head>No. 204.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wing to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Quito,
                                Ecuador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-10-10"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 10, 1873</date>.
                        (Received November 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 334.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I am gratified to ascertain that the
                    removal of all customs dues from American machinery is likely to prove a source
                    of considerable revenue to our countrymen engaged in that line of business, who
                    can now furnish such articles to Ecuadorian purchasers at cheaper rates than
                    their European competitors.</p>
                <p>I am already aware of several very large orders that will go forward to New York
                    at an early day; and I have no doubt that these will be largely increased in the
                    course of the coming year, owing to the great demand that is arising in all
                    parts of this country for machinery of all classes.</p>
                <p>As I expected, this action on the part of this government has created
                    considerable feeling in certain European quarters.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>RUMSEY WING.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
